{"text": "Mathematics is, in large part, the search for patterns, and for the explanations as to why those patterns exist. Such patterns indeed exist all around us — in nature, in our homes and schools, and in the motion of the sun, moon, and stars. They occur in everything that we do and see, from shopping and cooking, to throwing a ball and playing games, to understanding weather patterns and using technology.", "pdf_page": 1, "index_on_page": 0, "method": "graph", "cut_concepts": ["MATH6_C0002"], "chunk_id": "en-ch01-graph-p001-00", "language": "en", "chapter": 1, "chapter_title": "Patterns in Mathematics", "section": "1.1 What is Mathematics?", "printed_page": 1, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 405, "char_count": 405, "token_count": 96, "truncated_for_embedding": false, "concepts": ["MATH6_C0002"], "concept_names": ["pattern"], "primary_concept": "MATH6_C0002", "source_pdf": "fegp101.pdf", "source_clean_file": "assets/extracted/en/en_fegp101_clean.txt", "source_sha256": "704f65089ed97c3bb292a1b708021e321ecf6f68bb61e221b8b9420690b9e063", "citation_url": "/api/textbooks/en/fegp101.pdf#page=1", "method_params": {"max_chars": 450, "min_chars": 200}, "kg_version": "kg-v2-ch1-5", "metadata_version": "chunk-meta-v1"}
{"text": "The search for patterns and their explanations can be a fun and creative endeavour. It is for this reason that mathematicians think of mathematics both as an art and as a science. This year, we hope that you will get a chance to see the creativity and artistry involved in discovering and understanding mathematical patterns.", "pdf_page": 1, "index_on_page": 1, "method": "graph", "cut_concepts": ["MATH6_C0002"], "chunk_id": "en-ch01-graph-p001-01", "language": "en", "chapter": 1, "chapter_title": "Patterns in Mathematics", "section": "1.1 What is Mathematics?", "printed_page": 1, "char_start": 406, "char_end": 731, "char_count": 325, "token_count": 76, "truncated_for_embedding": false, "concepts": ["MATH6_C0002"], "concept_names": ["pattern"], "primary_concept": "MATH6_C0002", "source_pdf": "fegp101.pdf", "source_clean_file": "assets/extracted/en/en_fegp101_clean.txt", "source_sha256": "704f65089ed97c3bb292a1b708021e321ecf6f68bb61e221b8b9420690b9e063", "citation_url": "/api/textbooks/en/fegp101.pdf#page=1", "method_params": {"max_chars": 450, "min_chars": 200}, "kg_version": "kg-v2-ch1-5", "metadata_version": "chunk-meta-v1"}
{"text": "It is important to keep in mind that mathematics aims to not just find out what patterns exist, but also the explanations for why they exist. Such explanations can often then be used in applications well beyond the context in which they were discovered, which can then help to propel humanity forward.", "pdf_page": 1, "index_on_page": 2, "method": "graph", "cut_concepts": ["MATH6_C0002"], "chunk_id": "en-ch01-graph-p001-02", "language": "en", "chapter": 1, "chapter_title": "Patterns in Mathematics", "section": "1.1 What is Mathematics?", "printed_page": 1, "char_start": 732, "char_end": 1033, "char_count": 301, "token_count": 65, "truncated_for_embedding": false, "concepts": ["MATH6_C0002"], "concept_names": ["pattern"], "primary_concept": "MATH6_C0002", "source_pdf": "fegp101.pdf", "source_clean_file": "assets/extracted/en/en_fegp101_clean.txt", "source_sha256": "704f65089ed97c3bb292a1b708021e321ecf6f68bb61e221b8b9420690b9e063", "citation_url": "/api/textbooks/en/fegp101.pdf#page=1", "method_params": {"max_chars": 450, "min_chars": 200}, "kg_version": "kg-v2-ch1-5", "metadata_version": "chunk-meta-v1"}
