{"text": "3. How many little squares are there in each shape of the sequence of Stacked Squares? Which number sequence does this give? Can you explain why? 4. How many little triangles are there in each shape of the sequence of Stacked Triangles? Which number sequence does this give? Can you explain why? (Hint: In each shape in the sequence, how many triangles are there in each row?) 5.", "pdf_page": 12, "index_on_page": 0, "method": "graph", "cut_concepts": ["MATH6_C0005", "MATH6_C0009", "MATH6_C0107", "MATH6_C0108"], "chunk_id": "en-ch01-graph-p012-00", "language": "en", "chapter": 1, "chapter_title": "Patterns in Mathematics", "section": "1.6 Relation to Number Sequences", "printed_page": 12, "char_start": 0, "char_end": 379, "char_count": 379, "token_count": 104, "truncated_for_embedding": false, "concepts": ["MATH6_C0108", "MATH6_C0005", "MATH6_C0107", "MATH6_C0009"], "concept_names": ["stacked triangles", "number sequence", "stacked squares", "square numbers"], "primary_concept": "MATH6_C0108", "source_pdf": "fegp101.pdf", "source_clean_file": "assets/extracted/en/en_fegp101_clean.txt", "source_sha256": "704f65089ed97c3bb292a1b708021e321ecf6f68bb61e221b8b9420690b9e063", "citation_url": "/api/textbooks/en/fegp101.pdf#page=12", "method_params": {"max_chars": 450, "min_chars": 200}, "kg_version": "kg-v2-ch1-5", "metadata_version": "chunk-meta-v1"}
{"text": "To get from one shape to the next shape in the Koch Snowflake sequence, one replaces each line segment­ ‘—’ by a ‘speed bump’ . As one does this more and more times, the changes become tinier and tinier with very very small line segments. How many total line segments are there in each shape of the Koch Snowflake? What is the corresponding number sequence?", "pdf_page": 12, "index_on_page": 1, "method": "graph", "cut_concepts": ["MATH6_C0005", "MATH6_C0109"], "chunk_id": "en-ch01-graph-p012-01", "language": "en", "chapter": 1, "chapter_title": "Patterns in Mathematics", "section": "1.6 Relation to Number Sequences", "printed_page": 12, "char_start": 380, "char_end": 737, "char_count": 357, "token_count": 93, "truncated_for_embedding": false, "concepts": ["MATH6_C0005", "MATH6_C0109"], "concept_names": ["number sequence", "Koch snowflake"], "primary_concept": "MATH6_C0005", "source_pdf": "fegp101.pdf", "source_clean_file": "assets/extracted/en/en_fegp101_clean.txt", "source_sha256": "704f65089ed97c3bb292a1b708021e321ecf6f68bb61e221b8b9420690b9e063", "citation_url": "/api/textbooks/en/fegp101.pdf#page=12", "method_params": {"max_chars": 450, "min_chars": 200}, "kg_version": "kg-v2-ch1-5", "metadata_version": "chunk-meta-v1"}
{"text": "(The answer is 3, 12, 48, ..., i.e., 3 times Powers of 4; this sequence is not shown in Table 1.) Mathematics may be viewed as the search for patterns and for the explanations as to why those patterns exist. Among the most basic patterns that occur in mathematics are number sequences.", "pdf_page": 12, "index_on_page": 2, "method": "graph", "cut_concepts": ["MATH6_C0002", "MATH6_C0005"], "chunk_id": "en-ch01-graph-p012-02", "language": "en", "chapter": 1, "chapter_title": "Patterns in Mathematics", "section": "1.6 Relation to Number Sequences", "printed_page": 12, "char_start": 738, "char_end": 1023, "char_count": 285, "token_count": 82, "truncated_for_embedding": false, "concepts": ["MATH6_C0005", "MATH6_C0002"], "concept_names": ["number sequence", "pattern"], "primary_concept": "MATH6_C0005", "source_pdf": "fegp101.pdf", "source_clean_file": "assets/extracted/en/en_fegp101_clean.txt", "source_sha256": "704f65089ed97c3bb292a1b708021e321ecf6f68bb61e221b8b9420690b9e063", "citation_url": "/api/textbooks/en/fegp101.pdf#page=12", "method_params": {"max_chars": 450, "min_chars": 200}, "kg_version": "kg-v2-ch1-5", "metadata_version": "chunk-meta-v1"}
{"text": "Some important examples of number sequences include the counting numbers, odd numbers, even numbers, square numbers, triangular numbers, cube numbers, Virahānka numbers, and powers of 2. Sometimes number sequences can be related to each other in beautiful and remarkable ways. For example, adding up the sequence of odd numbers starting with 1 gives square numbers.", "pdf_page": 12, "index_on_page": 3, "method": "graph", "cut_concepts": ["MATH6_ADD001", "MATH6_C0005", "MATH6_C0006", "MATH6_C0007", "MATH6_C0008", "MATH6_C0009", "MATH6_C0010", "MATH6_C0011", "MATH6_C0105"], "chunk_id": "en-ch01-graph-p012-03", "language": "en", "chapter": 1, "chapter_title": "Patterns in Mathematics", "section": "1.6 Relation to Number Sequences", "printed_page": 12, "char_start": 1024, "char_end": 1389, "char_count": 365, "token_count": 86, "truncated_for_embedding": false, "concepts": ["MATH6_C0008", "MATH6_ADD001", "MATH6_C0013", "MATH6_C0005", "MATH6_C0009", "MATH6_C0007", "MATH6_C0010", "MATH6_C0011", "MATH6_C0006", "MATH6_C0105"], "concept_names": ["triangular numbers", "Virahānka numbers", "counting numbers", "number sequence", "square numbers", "even numbers", "cube numbers", "powers of 2", "odd numbers", "relations among number sequences"], "primary_concept": "MATH6_C0008", "source_pdf": "fegp101.pdf", "source_clean_file": "assets/extracted/en/en_fegp101_clean.txt", "source_sha256": "704f65089ed97c3bb292a1b708021e321ecf6f68bb61e221b8b9420690b9e063", "citation_url": "/api/textbooks/en/fegp101.pdf#page=12", "method_params": {"max_chars": 450, "min_chars": 200}, "kg_version": "kg-v2-ch1-5", "metadata_version": "chunk-meta-v1"}
{"text": "Visualising number sequences using pictures can help to understand sequences and the relationships between them. Shape sequences are another basic type of pattern in mathematics. Some important examples of shape sequences include regular polygons, complete graphs, stacked triangles and squares, and Koch snowflake iterations. Shape sequences also exhibit many interesting relationships with number sequences.", "pdf_page": 12, "index_on_page": 4, "method": "graph", "cut_concepts": ["MATH6_C0002", "MATH6_C0004", "MATH6_C0005", "MATH6_C0009", "MATH6_C0014", "MATH6_C0015", "MATH6_C0106", "MATH6_C0108", "MATH6_C0109"], "chunk_id": "en-ch01-graph-p012-04", "language": "en", "chapter": 1, "chapter_title": "Patterns in Mathematics", "section": "1.6 Relation to Number Sequences", "printed_page": 12, "char_start": 1390, "char_end": 1799, "char_count": 409, "token_count": 95, "truncated_for_embedding": false, "concepts": ["MATH6_C0108", "MATH6_C0005", "MATH6_C0014", "MATH6_C0004", "MATH6_C0106", "MATH6_C0109", "MATH6_C0015", "MATH6_C0002", "MATH6_C0009"], "concept_names": ["stacked triangles", "number sequence", "regular polygons", "shape sequences", "complete graphs", "Koch snowflake", "visualising number sequences", "pattern", "square numbers"], "primary_concept": "MATH6_C0108", "source_pdf": "fegp101.pdf", "source_clean_file": "assets/extracted/en/en_fegp101_clean.txt", "source_sha256": "704f65089ed97c3bb292a1b708021e321ecf6f68bb61e221b8b9420690b9e063", "citation_url": "/api/textbooks/en/fegp101.pdf#page=12", "method_params": {"max_chars": 450, "min_chars": 200}, "kg_version": "kg-v2-ch1-5", "metadata_version": "chunk-meta-v1"}
